[38;5;231m⏺[39m Fixed.

  [1mRoot cause:[0m [38;5;153msrc/pricing.js:15[39m — [38;5;153mgetDiscountRate[39m returned [38;5;153mRATES[code][39m, which is [38;5;153mundefined[39m for any code not in the
  table. [38;5;153mfinalPrice[39m then computed [38;5;153mprice - price * undefined[39m → [38;5;153mNaN[39m, which [38;5;153mtoFixed(2)[39m rendered as [38;5;153m"NaN"[39m. The [38;5;153mRATES[39m table
  has a null prototype, so a miss is always a genuine unknown code (even [38;5;153m'toString'[39m misses); the only defect was the
  absent "no discount" value.

  [1mFix:[0m one change at the source — [38;5;153mreturn RATES[code] ?? 0;[39m. Fixing it in [38;5;153mgetDiscountRate[39m rather than in [38;5;153mfinalPrice[39m means
  any future caller of the lookup gets a usable number too. Used [38;5;153m??[39m rather than [38;5;153m||[39m so a legitimately zero-valued rate
  added later would still be honored.

  [1mVerification:[0m Added [38;5;153mtest/pricing.test.js[39m using Node's built-in runner (the repo had no test framework). Tests failed
  before the fix (3 of 4) and all 4 pass after; the original receipt now prints [38;5;153mTotal: $100.00[39m. Run with [38;5;153mnode --test[39m.

  One note: I didn't add a [38;5;153mtest[39m script to [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m since the repo had no testing setup at all — say the word if you
  want one wired up.

